"On the SPOT: Enhancing Speculative Decodingfor Diffusion Language Modelsvia Commit-Order Prediction (https://arxiv.org/html/2610.22543)\nciteturn41266view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.22543\",\"lineno\":185}); Total lines: 1284\nL175: Because attention inside a block is bidirectional, the target decides not only what to write but where, so the commit order is an extra dimension of decoding and speculative decoding does not transfer directly. For verification we build on communication-free coupling: cite141†Daliri et al. (2025) show that two parties sampling from $P$ and $Q$ under shared Gumbel noise agree with probability at least $(1-D_{TV})/(1+D_{TV})$, and apply this to drafter-invariant speculative decoding.\nL176: We carry the construction to diffusion decoding, where per-position shared noise makes the target’s sampled commitment at any hypothetical block state a deterministic function of that state (Section cite21†4.3 ).\nL177: #### Speculative decoding for diffusion targets.\nL178: SSD (cite128†Gao et al., 2025 ) and Spiffy (cite129†Agrawal et al., 2026 ) study speculative decoding with DLMs targets: SSD orders positions by confidence into a self-drafted chain and verifies it greedily, whereas Spiffy drafts states along an offline-calibrated graph and verifies them blockwise. Both are training-free and greedy.\nL179: MRP (cite123†Xu et al., 2026 ) learns a residual head that extrapolates the target’s logits a few denoising steps ahead and accepts drafted tokens in one pass, greedy only (Section cite29†5.4 sets its reported gains beside ours). BlockSpec (cite124†Pan et al., 2025 ) extends training-free self-speculation to trees on decoding trajectories, and SimSD (cite130†Cui et al., 2026 ) drafts with a same-family sibling model but verifies approximately, so it is not lossless and is not added to our comparison.\nL180: In contrast, our drafter learns the commit order, builds multi-step trees, verifies exactly under sampling, and transfers across targets without retraining.\nL181: ## 3 Analysis of Block-Diffusion Speculative Decoding\nL182: #### Setting.\nL183: The target decodes a block of $L$ masked positions one commit per forward. A draft is a hypothetical block state with a few more positions filled by the drafter, and $D$ drafts are verified at once by appending each behind the cached prefix as its own copy of the block, so a single forward over $(1{+}D)L$ positions returns the target’s next commit at the root and at every draft. A draft is accepted iff it equals that commit at its parent, so the accepted set is one path and the output cannot change.\nL184: A draft therefore costs a whole block rather than a token: budgets are small, and target forwards (NFE) and wall clock diverge (Appendix cite70†D.2 ). Two measurements on SDAR-8B shape the method: what a drafter must predict, and how its proposals must be verified once the target samples.\nL185: ### 3.1 Draft-Trajectory Dynamics\nL186: \nL187: A draft must predict which position the target commits next and what token it writes. To isolate their value we evaluate live oracles that peek at the target continuation and propose (i) true positions filled with stale-argmax tokens, (ii) true tokens ordered by stale confidence, or (iii) both; each bounds the gain obtainable from one information source under standard verification.\nL188: Figure 2: (a) Live oracle ceilings on SDAR-8B (b32): peak throughput ratios over draft-free decoding; positions are worth more than tokens, and a perfect drafter reaches $3.8$–$4.6\\times$ (full grid in Table cite142†7 ). (b) Stale coverage: probability that all committed tokens over the next $K$ steps lie within the stale top-$t$ candidates.\nL189: Figure cite143†2 (a) and Table cite142†7 (Appendix cite82†F.2 ) show that position knowledge dominates on every task and keeps paying across the tested budgets (NFE reduction $5.4\\times\\to 7.1\\times$ from $D8$ to $D20$ on GSM8K, $5.7\\times\\to 8.1\\times$ on MBPP), whereas true tokens yield only $3.3$–$6.5\\times$ at $D20$.\nL190: The stale distributions already carry token identity: the stale argmax matches the true token in $92\\%$ of first-block commits and $\\geq 90\\%$ thereafter, and eight stale candidates per position cover $89\\%$ of entire eight-commit continuations (Figure cite143†2 (b)). They fail to predict order: the probability that the previous round’s top-confidence position commits next drops to $3\\%$ by block $9$ (Appendix cite93†F.8 ). What the model writes is nearly known; which position it writes next is not.\nL191: NFE does not map directly to wall clock, since every drafted round forwards $(1{+}D)L$ positions, so we charge each oracle its measured verification cost (lookahead subtracted); under this accounting the joint oracle peaks at $3.8$–$4.6\\times$ (at an interior budget, $D12$) against an NFE ceiling of $8.0$–$9.7\\times$.\nL192: By learning the missing order, SPOT predicts the next commit with $75\\%$ accuracy on held-out trajectories (Appendix cite68†D.1 ) against $22\\%$ for the stale-confidence order, recovering $70$–$85\\%$ of the position oracle’s NFE reduction and $54$–$81\\%$ of the joint wall-clock ceiling (Table cite144†2 ). Commit-order prediction is therefore the primary information bottleneck for drafting on this target, token knowledge a complementary second axis, and verification cost a separate limit on realized speed.\nL193: ### 3.2 Verification Dynamics Across Sampling Temperatures\nL194: At $T{=}0$ the acceptance test above is state equality against a deterministic commit.\nL195: At $T{>}0$ the commitment is a random draw, and there are three ways to keep verifying by equality: a greedy match accepts a drafted state iff it equals the target’s argmax commitment, as the $T{=}0$ verifier would if simply run at temperature; an uncoupled match lets the target sample independently and accepts only if the sample happens to equal the proposal; a coupled-noise match shares the sampler’s Gumbel noise between drafter and verifier, so that the target’s draw is again a deterministic function of the state (Section cite21†4.3 ).\nL196: We ran all three on SPOT, Spiffy and the BlockSpec-style tree at $T\\in\\{0.5,1,1.5,2,2.5\\}$ (Table cite145†1 , Appendix cite88†F.6 ).\nL197: Table 1: Temperature sweep on SDAR-8B MBPP. Draft-free columns: target accuracy, argmax fraction, mean committed-token probability. SPOT $D8$: NFE and throughput against the $T$-matched draft-free control. Greedy-match columns show distribution drift. The $D8$ NFE standard deviation over seeds grows from $0.3$ at $T{=}0.5$ to $8.5$ at $T{=}2.5$. ^{†}Greedy match is not distribution-preserving, so its NFE is not comparable.\nL198:  | draft-free ($D0$)  | SPOT $D8$ NFE (tok/s ratio)  | greedy SPOT  | Spiffy $D8$ NFE\nL199: --- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nL200: $T$  | acc  | argmax  | mean $p$  | coupled  | uncoupled  | greedy^{†}  | acc  | argmax  | coupled  | uncoupled\nL201: --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nL202: 0.5  | 0.35  | 0.986  | 0.993  | 58.2 (2.56)  | 58.2 (2.57)  | 57.6 (2.57)  | 0.36  | 0.978  | 84.3  | 84.0\nL203: 1.0  | 0.34  | 0.976  | 0.965  | 60.1 (2.45)  | 60.2 (2.43)  | 56.8 (2.55)  | 0.38  | 0.973  | 86.3  | 86.6\nL204: 1.5  | 0.08  | 0.866  | 0.828  | 74.0 (1.97)  | 82.6 (1.77)  | 57.4 (2.55)  | 0.28  | 0.958  | 94.2  | 108.9\nL205: 2.0  | 0.00  | 0.057  | 0.052  | 187.4 (0.78)  | 256.7 (0.54)  | 94.7 (1.52)  | 0.01  | 0.750  | 114.6  | 258.5\nL206: 2.5  | 0.00  | 0.001  | 0.000  | 200.8 (0.71)  | 269.1 (0.54)  | 128.1 (1.13)  | 0.00  | 0.582  | 110.5  | 269.2\nL207: (i) Coupled and uncoupled match are exact; greedy match is not. In both exact regimes the emitted tokens track the draft-free statistics within sampling noise: over all three drafters at $D8$ and $D20$ and $T\\leq 1.5$ (Table cite146†21 ), the emitted argmax fraction stays within $0.016$ of the draft-free value, the mean committed-token probability within $0.015$, and accuracy within $0.04$.\nL208: Greedy match drifts, and the drift grows with $T$: at $T{=}1.5$ it emits accuracy $0.28$ where the model samples $0.08$ and picks the argmax at $95.8\\%$ of commits against the model’s $86.6\\%$; the drift is proportional to acceptance, so it makes the best drafter the most biased. (ii) Coupling keeps acceptance alive as the target’s samples move away from its argmax.\nL209: At $T\\leq 1$ the sampled token is the argmax $98$–$99\\%$ of the time and the two verifiers agree within $1\\%$ in NFE; at $T{=}1.5$ uncoupled costs $12$–$16\\%$ more NFE for SPOT and Spiffy ($74.0\\to 82.6$ and $94.2\\to 108.9$); at $T{=}2$ uncoupled accepts almost nothing ($256.7$ NFE against $269.2$ draft-free) while coupled still drafts. The mechanism is the coupling bound of 【141†Daliri et al.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPredicting Privileged Information Effectiveness for On-Policy Self-Distillation (https://arxiv.org/html/2610.22512v1)\nciteturn41266view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.22512v1\",\"lineno\":150}); Total lines: 1041\nL130: Beyond downstream performance, we evaluate three offline metrics: PI utility [cite95†19 ] compares teacher reward with and without PI, but can be distorted by information leakage; sampled-token reverse KL $D$ measures the PI-induced probability shift on student-sampled tokens; and error-step KL contrast $\\Delta d^{-}_{\\mathrm{err}}$ measures whether PI suppresses tokens more at identified errors than at matched steps from the same task.\nL131: Together, these experiments ask not only which PI works, but also what distinguishes useful PI from information that merely makes the teacher more capable when PI is present.\nL132: Our results show that PI effectiveness depends on its content, source, and the student model. PI formats with information-dense trajectories usually outperform compressed hints, and adding environment observations consistently improves the Instruct students on ALFWorld and AppWorld. Additional teacher reasoning is less reliable and can reduce the gains of the thinking models.\nL133: Among hint formats, skills are generally strongest, suggesting that additional guidance, such as an analysis of mistakes and reusable strategies, is more effective than a plain trajectory summary. More capable generators also tend to produce more effective PI, particularly for hint formats, showing that PI quality depends on both its format and source.\nL134: Among the three offline metrics, error-step KL contrast better distinguishes useful PI and improves per-task selection when PI candidates vary in source or strength. Sampled-token reverse KL tracks performance in eight of nine settings but does not capture the direction of the shift, while PI utility is unreliable because of information leakage (Figure cite113†3 ).\nL135: Our controlled evaluations on ALFWorld also show that PI utility can be distorted by information leakage and that reverse KL can assign large values to unhelpful shifts. Using the most effective PI format, we further show that combining OPSD with GRPO can outperform a strong GRPO baseline.\nL136: Our main contributions are:\nL137: \nL138:   * •\nL139: \nL140: Systematic study of PI for OPSD. To our knowledge, this is the first controlled study of PI construction for OPSD, spanning 16 PI configurations and three student models across three multi-turn agentic benchmarks.\nL141: \nL142:   * •\nL143: \nL144: Practical PI design recommendations. We identify how PI content, format, generation source, and student-model choice shape OPSD gains.\nL145: \nL146:   * •\nL147: KL-based PI assessment and selection. We propose two offline KL diagnostics, evaluate what they measure through controlled studies, and show that error-step KL contrast can guide per-task PI selection before costly OPSD training.\nL148: \nL149:   * •\nL150: \nL151: Combining OPSD with GRPO. We show that the most effective PI enables combined OPSD and GRPO training to outperform matched GRPO baselines.\nL152: ## 2 Analyzing Privileged Information for OPSD\nL153: ### 2.1 On-Policy Self-Distillation and PI Construction\nL154: On-policy distillation (OPD) transfers knowledge from a teacher on trajectories generated by the student, providing token-level supervision on the states that the student actually visits [cite114†1 , cite115†31 ]. OPSD instantiates this approach without a separate teacher model: the student and teacher share parameters, but only the teacher observes PI. Let $\\theta$ denote their shared parameters, $I$ the PI, and $\\pi_{\\theta}^{S}$ and $\\pi_{\\theta}^{T}$ the student and PI-conditioned teacher policies.\nL155: For a student trajectory $\\tau=(y_{1},\\ldots,y_{L_{\\tau}})$, $s_{t}$ is the context before token $y_{t}$, and $L_{\\tau}$ is the trajectory length. OPSD minimizes the reverse KL from the student to the PI-conditioned teacher:\nL156:  | $$\\mathcal{L}_{\\mathrm{OPSD}}(\\theta)=\\mathbb{E}_{\\tau\\sim\\pi_{\\theta}^{S}}\\left[\\frac{1}{L_{\\tau}}\\sum_{t=1}^{L_{\\tau}}D_{\\mathrm{KL}}\\!\\left(\\pi_{\\theta}^{S}(\\cdot\\mid s_{t})\\,\\|\\,\\operatorname{sg}\\!\\left[\\pi_{\\theta}^{T}(\\cdot\\mid s_{t},I)\\right]\\right)\\right].$$  |  | (1)\nL157: \nL158: Here, $D_{\\mathrm{KL}}$ compares the two next-token distributions, and $\\operatorname{sg}$ stops gradients through the teacher.\nL159: This formulation has been applied with ground-truth answers [cite96†35 ], reflective environment feedback [cite97†7 ], and information retained across continual-learning tasks [cite98†22 ]. Because the two policies differ only in access to PI, PI construction directly determines the token-level training signal [cite95†19 ].\nL160: We study seven PI formats constructed using GLM-5.2 and Qwen3.7-Plus. The formats comprise four trajectory variants and three types of hints. Figure cite111†1 illustrates these formats and the overall OPSD pipeline. Appendix cite48†A outlines the PI construction protocol.\nL161: ##### Trajectories.\nL162: Following cite95†Penaloza et al. [19] , we sample successful reference trajectories from the expert models and retain different components: actions only (Action), actions with environment observations (Action $+$ Obs.), actions with reasoning (Action $+$ Thou.), or actions, observations, and reasoning (Action $+$ Obs. $+$ Thou.). These variants isolate whether effective PI requires only the expert’s actions, the environmental context surrounding those actions, or the reasoning used to derive them.\nL163: ##### Summary and Skill Hints.\nL164: \nL165: Summary hints condense a reference trajectory into a brief description of its solution [cite95†19 ]. Skill hints provide more detailed guidance by combining high-level strategies, mistake analysis, and reusable skills derived from the reference trajectory [cite101†26 ]. We also use the student model itself to generate both types of hints.\nL166: ##### Checklist Hints.\nL167: \nL168: We generate checklists from a successful reference and a failed student trajectory, listing task requirements for the teacher. Like rubric-conditioned self-distillation [cite102†20 , cite103†6 ], this format provides structured criteria rather than a target response.\nL169: ### 2.2 Offline Metrics\nL170: \nL171: Training a separate student for every PI candidate is computationally expensive. We therefore study offline metrics that can assess PI effectiveness before training and help identify what distinguishes PI that produces better learning signals for the student policy.\nL172: \nL173: Using the notation above, let $R(\\tau)\\in\\{0,1\\}$ denote whether trajectory $\\tau$ completes its task successfully. We define the following metrics:\nL174: ##### PI utility.\nL175: \nL176: PI utility measures the improvement in task success obtained by exposing the same model to PI:\nL177: \nL178:  | $$U(I)=\\mathbb{E}_{\\tau\\sim\\pi_{\\theta}^{T}(\\cdot\\mid I)}[R(\\tau)]-\\mathbb{E}_{\\tau\\sim\\pi_{\\theta}^{S}}[R(\\tau)].$$  |  | (2)\nL179: \nL180: A positive value indicates that the teacher solves more tasks when conditioned on PI, motivating the hypothesis that higher PI utility leads to more effective OPSD training.\nL181: ##### Sampled-token Reverse KL.\nL182: \nL183: For a student trajectory $\\tau=(y_{1},\\ldots,y_{L_{\\tau}})$, we estimate the reverse KL from the sampled-token log-ratio, using its absolute magnitude so that shifts in either direction count:\nL184: \nL185:  | $$d_{I}(\\tau)=\\frac{1}{L_{\\tau}}\\sum_{t=1}^{L_{\\tau}}\\left|\\log\\frac{\\pi_{\\theta}^{S}(y_{t}\\mid s_{t})}{\\pi_{\\theta}^{T}(y_{t}\\mid s_{t},I)}\\right|,\\qquad y_{t}\\sim\\pi_{\\theta}^{S}(\\cdot\\mid s_{t}).$$  |  | (3)\nL186: Intuitively, a larger reverse KL indicates that PI more strongly changes the teacher’s predictions on student-visited states, providing a stronger potential learning signal. We report $D(I)=\\mathbb{E}_{\\tau\\sim\\pi_{\\theta}^{S}}[d_{I}(\\tau)]$ over the sampled student trajectories.\nL187: ##### Error-step KL Contrast ($\\Delta d^{-}_{\\mathrm{err}}$).\nL188: \nL189: This metric measures whether PI suppresses tokens more strongly at identified errors than at comparable steps in other rollouts. An agent trajectory contains multiple interaction steps, each of which may contain several generated tokens. For step $j$, let $\\mathcal{T}_{j}(\\tau)\\subseteq\\{1,\\ldots,L_{\\tau}\\}$ denote its token indices and define\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nDon’t Retrain, Remix: Adaptation of Web Agents using Mixture Hypernetworks (https://arxiv.org/html/2610.22539v1)\nciteturn41266view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.22539v1\",\"lineno\":240}); Total lines: 600\nL220: We evaluate on (i) TimeWarp (TW) (cite69†Ishmam and Marino, 2026 ), a containerized web benchmark with three domains (Wiki, News, Shop), each rendered in six versions, and (ii) WebArena-Lite-v2 (WAL) (cite93†Liu et al., 2026 ), an updated version of WebArena-Lite (cite92†Liu et al., 2024 ), itself a curated subset of WebArena (cite87†Zhou et al., 2024 ), with five domains (Reddit, GitLab, CMS, Map, OSS).\nL221: We evaluate domain transfer, in which the unseen interface is a new domain, on both benchmarks, and version transfer, in which it is a new version, on TW, holding out v1 and v5 in turn. We conduct all the experiments with the BrowserUse harness (cite74†Müller and Žunič, 2024 ), with non-rationale tokens and memory enabled.\nL222: ##### Baselines.\nL223: We compare WebMix with three baselines on two open-weight models, Qwen-3.5 4B (cite78†Qwen Team, 2026 ) and Llama-3.1 8B (cite77†Grattafiori et al., 2024 ): the base model zero-shot, in-context learning (ICL) (cite121†Brown et al., 2020 ), and LoRA (cite79†Hu et al., 2022 ). We evaluate each method in two settings. In the U nseen setting, the evaluated domain or version is left out of the training data and the in-context examples. In the S een setting, it is included.\nL224: In the seen setting, we report LoRA trained only on the evaluated interface (in), which is a target-trained specialist, and LoRA trained on all interfaces (all). Each number is the average of three runs. § cite40†A.4 gives the training details.\nL225:  |  |  | Domains  | Versions\nL226: Model  | Method  | Wiki  | News  | Shop  | Avg.  | v1  | v5  | Avg.\nL227: Qwen-3.5 4B  | Zero-shot  | 19.35  | 30.30  | 20.99  | 22.92  | 17.48  | 19.09  | 18.28\nL228: U  | In-context Learning  | 18.28  | 40.91  | 20.99  | 25.42  | 19.74  | 20.39  | 20.06\nL229: LoRA  | 17.20  | 45.45  | 18.52  | 25.42  | 26.54  | 22.01  | 24.27\nL230: WebMix  | 