ns on TW and WAL, respectively. WebMix outperforms every baseline on both models and transfer types, often matching the performance of baselines trained on those interfaces. For example, it matches LoRA (in) on TW domains with both models. Gains are largest for domain transfer, at 7.5–8.3 percentage points (pp) over the best baseline on TW and 3.3–4.6pp on WAL.\nL275: Version transfer gains are smaller (1.8–3.2pp), and LoRA is a strong baseline there. Transfer is strongest for domains that are similar to the training domains. With Qwen-3.5, News gains 26pp over zero-shot, compared to $\\sim$5pp on Wiki and Shop. On WAL, OSS gains the most for both models (14–15pp). The one failure case is Map with Llama-3.1, where every unseen method scores zero. Composing adapters trained on other interfaces thus recovers most of the benefit of training on the target itself.\nL276: ##### RQ2: Retention on seen interfaces.\nL277: \nL278: WebMix’s transfer gains do not come at the cost of S een interface performance. In Tables cite123†1 and cite124†2 , WebMix is the best or tied-best method in every column. It improves over the strongest seen baseline, LoRA (all), by 4.6–6.3pp on TW domains, 1.8–2.4pp on TW versions, and 2.6–4.3pp on WAL. Training LoRA on all domains adds only 0.2–1.3pp over in-domain training, suggesting that WebMix’s gains do not solely come from more training data.\nL279: ##### RQ3: Design choices.\nL280: Hypernetwork design. Table cite125†3 isolates the contribution of each component. T2L Recons (Appendix cite47†B.2 ), which regresses the stored adapter updates, is the weakest variant. T2L SFT (Appendix cite48†B.3 ), which trains the same generator through the policy, improves on it by 9.17pp. Replacing weight generation with the mixture hypernetwork (§cite16†3.2 ) adds a further 3.75pp, mostly from News (+9pp).\nL281: Conditioning the router on the scout then adds another 4.58pp, for a total gain of 8.33pp over the strongest T2L variant. Composing a frozen bank is easier to learn from a handful of interfaces than generating weights. Appendix cite56†D.2 analyzes an idealization of T2L Recons. Scouting budget. Scout outperforms Mixture on average for every number of scouted tasks $n$, but $n{=}1$ is best. Larger $n$ gives a marginal gain on News (+1.5pp at $n{=}3$) but costs 6–8 points on Wiki, so we use $n{=}1$ throughout.\n"
